==================================================================
DAY 11 - PART B: LAB
Create, scale, and roll out a Deployment
Time: 75-90 minutes
==================================================================

cd ~/k8s-journey/manifests/week2


STEP 1: Generate and write a Deployment
---------------------------------------
k create deployment web --image=nginx:1.26 --replicas=3 --dry-run=client -o yaml > deploy-web.yaml

Edit deploy-web.yaml: remove "creationTimestamp", "resources: {}" and
"status: {}", and add a strategy block under spec:

  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxSurge: 1
      maxUnavailable: 0

k apply -f deploy-web.yaml
k get deploy,rs,pods
k get deploy web -o wide

Read the columns: READY, UP-TO-DATE, AVAILABLE.


STEP 2: See the 3 layers
------------------------
k describe deploy web | head -25
k get rs -l app=web
k get pods -l app=web --show-labels       # note pod-template-hash


STEP 3: Scale
-------------
k scale deploy web --replicas=6
k get pods
k scale deploy web --replicas=3
k rollout history deploy/web              # still only 1 revision


STEP 4: Rolling update, watched live
------------------------------------
Terminal 2:
  k get rs -l app=web -w
Terminal 3:
  k get pods -l app=web -w

Terminal 1:
  k set image deploy/web nginx=nginx:1.27
  k rollout status deploy/web

(The container name is "nginx" because "k create deployment" names it
after the image. Check with: k get deploy web -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].name}')

Watch: a new ReplicaSet appears and scales up one pod at a time while
the old one scales down. Afterwards:
  k get rs -l app=web        # old RS at 0, new RS at 3


STEP 5: Prove no downtime (traffic during update)
-------------------------------------------------
Create a temporary Service so we can send traffic (Services: Week 3):
  k expose deploy web --port=80

Start a client pod that requests the service in a loop:
  k run client --image=busybox:1.36 -- sh -c 'while true; do wget -qO- -T 1 web >/dev/null && echo OK || echo FAIL; sleep 0.3; done'
  k logs -f client

In another terminal, roll out a change:
  k set env deploy/web DEMO=1
  k rollout status deploy/web

Watch the client log. You should see only (or almost only) OK.
(A few FAILs are possible without readiness probes and preStop delays;
Week 5 makes it perfect.)


STEP 6: Recreate strategy (compare)
-----------------------------------
k patch deploy web -p '{"spec":{"strategy":{"type":"Recreate","rollingUpdate":null}}}'
k set image deploy/web nginx=nginx:1.26
k get pods -l app=web -w

All old pods terminate first, then new ones start. Watch the client
log: FAIL lines appear during the gap.

Switch back:
  k patch deploy web -p '{"spec":{"strategy":{"type":"RollingUpdate","rollingUpdate":{"maxSurge":1,"maxUnavailable":0}}}}'


STEP 7: Rollout restart
-----------------------
k rollout restart deploy/web
k get pods -l app=web

All pods replaced with the same image. Useful after a ConfigMap change
(Week 4) or to clear a bad state.


STEP 8: Pause and resume (batch several changes into one rollout)
-----------------------------------------------------------------
k rollout pause deploy/web
k set image deploy/web nginx=nginx:1.27
k set env deploy/web TEAM=platform
k get rs -l app=web                       # nothing new yet
k rollout resume deploy/web
k rollout status deploy/web


STEP 9: Make the YAML the source of truth
-----------------------------------------
Your live object has drifted from deploy-web.yaml. Update the file
(image nginx:1.27, env vars) and run:
  k diff -f deploy-web.yaml
Repeat until diff shows only harmless differences. Lesson: in real
teams, every change goes through the file, not "k set".


STEP 10: Clean up (keep the YAML) and commit
--------------------------------------------
k delete pod client
k delete svc web
k delete deploy web
cd ~/k8s-journey && git add . && git commit -m "day11: deployments"
